// #1511 / #2121: the `handle` eligibility boundary, pinned per callee shape.
//
// docs/cheatsheet.md described this boundary in prose and drifted: it recorded
// a local-closure callee as REJECTED when it compiles, and the diagnostic text
// still claims a closure-parameter call is rejected when it is accepted. Prose
// cannot be checked; these can.
effect Ask { Get() -> Int }

fn ask_once() -> Int with Ask { perform Ask::Get() }

// The callee is a LOCAL CLOSURE with no effect row.
//
// The `ok` verdict recorded for this fixture is TRUE OF THIS LANE ONLY, and the
// distinction matters: expected.tsv compiles every fixture with `__no_entry__`,
// which skips the entry-boundary lowering where handle eligibility is decided.
// `vibe check` REJECTS this program:
//
//   error: handle of effect 'Ask' cannot be compiled here: the handled body
//   calls 'bump', a local binding whose type carries no effect row
//
// An earlier version of this comment said "measured, it compiles" without that
// caveat, because it had been measured through this lane. The real boundary --
// including the three rejected shapes and the two edits that fix them -- is
// pinned by scripts/check_handle_eligibility.sh, which compiles with a real
// entry name. See #2137.
fn main() -> Unit {
  let bump = (n: Int) -> Int { n + 1 }
  let _ = handle { bump(ask_once()) } with Ask { Get() => resume(1) }
}
